    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the concept.\n\n**CSRF fundamentals** is a CSRF-defense topic. Explain how browser credentials are attached, how an attacker could cause a state change, and which same-site, token, origin, or request-metadata control prevents it.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the asset, attacker capability, trust boundary, secure default, enforcement point, and failure response before writing code.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core decision or policy check so you can explain the security reasoning line by line.\n\n```ts\ninterface RequestState {\n  csrfToken: string;\n}\n\nfunction buildRequestToken(\n  state: RequestState,\n): string {\n  return state.csrfToken;\n}\n\nconst token = buildRequestToken({\n  csrfToken: \"server-issued-token\",\n});\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or browser APIs when they make the same security decision clearer without hiding the important control.\n\n```ts\nconst form = new FormData();\n\nform.set(\"csrfToken\", \"server-issued-token\");\n\nconst token = form.get(\"csrfToken\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse CSRF fundamentals on state-changing requests when browser-managed credentials are involved. Verify the defense server-side and test both accepted and rejected requests.\n\nStep 6 — Validation:\nTest the allowed path, denied path, malformed input, missing credential, expired state, and misconfiguration that could broaden access.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain what is trusted, what is untrusted, where the final enforcement happens, what the secure default is, and how the control is monitored and migrated.",
